      "part5": [
        {
          "pairedMetric": "Trade Weighted Index",
          "why": "The trade-weighted index covers a broader set of trading-partner currencies while the dollar index focuses on major developed currencies, so reading them together reveals whether dollar strength is broad-based or concentrated against the majors. This distinction affects which multinationals are most exposed. This separates broad dollar strength from major-currency-specific strength.",
          "sharpens": "When both indexes are rising together, dollar strength is broad and all multinationals and commodity producers face headwinds, which favors domestic-focused sectors across the board. When the dollar index is rising but the trade-weighted index is flatter, the dollar is strong only against major currencies and companies with emerging-market exposure may be less affected, allowing more selective positioning within global sectors."
        },
        {
          "pairedMetric": "10-year, 2-year, 3-month rate",
          "why": "Interest rate differentials between the US and other developed economies are a primary driver of the dollar, so reading the dollar alongside US rates confirms whether the currency move is rate-driven. Rate-driven dollar moves tend to persist while sentiment-driven moves can reverse. This connects the currency to its main fundamental driver.",
          "sharpens": "When the dollar is rising AND US rates are rising relative to abroad, the dollar strength is fundamentally supported and likely to persist, which sustains the headwind on multinationals and commodities and favors domestic sectors. When the dollar is rising but US rates are stable, the move may be driven by safe-haven flows that could reverse, which argues for caution before fully committing to domestic-focused positioning."
        },
        {
          "pairedMetric": "Oil (WTI Crude)",
          "why": "Oil is priced in dollars, so the dollar's strength directly affects the dollar oil price, and reading them together separates a genuine oil supply-demand move from a currency effect. A weaker dollar lifts oil prices independent of oil fundamentals. This isolates the currency effect on oil.",
          "sharpens": "When the dollar is falling AND oil is rising, part of the oil strength is currency-driven, which supports Energy producers but means the oil rise may not reflect genuine demand tightness. When the dollar is rising AND oil is still rising, the oil strength is genuine demand or supply tightness overcoming a currency headwind, which is a stronger signal for the Energy sector."
        }
      ]
