        {
          "pairedMetric": "Investment-Grade Credit Spread (OAS)",
          "why": "The Baa spread isolates the borderline tier over a century of history while the broad investment-grade OAS prices the whole high-grade market with more modeling precision over a shorter window, so reading them together combines long-run regime context with a precise current read. The two together confirm whether the lowest investment-grade tier is leading or lagging the broad market. This blends historical perspective with present-day accuracy.",
          "sharpens": "When the broad investment-grade spread is calm and the Baa spread is also narrow by historical standards, the borderline tier is safe and cyclical exposure can be held. When both widen and the Baa spread reaches levels high by its own long history, the borderline tier is under real stress, which confirms a shift toward a defensive regime."
        },
        {
          "pairedMetric": "High-Yield Credit Spread (OAS)",
          "why": "The Baa spread prices the last rung of investment grade while the high-yield spread prices the tier just below it, so reading them together shows whether stress is crossing the investment-grade cutoff in either direction. The gap between them measures how the market prices that critical border. This tracks pressure right at the line where forced selling begins.",
          "sharpens": "When the high-yield spread widens but the Baa spread stays narrow, stress is contained below the cutoff and only the weakest names are affected. When both widen and the Baa spread climbs toward the high-yield tier, the border is under pressure and fallen-angel risk is rising, which favors avoiding the most leveraged names and rotating toward defensives."
        },
        {
          "pairedMetric": "10-Year Treasury Yield",
          "why": "The Baa spread is measured over the 10-year Treasury, so reading them together separates the credit-risk component from the underlying cost of safe long-term money. A rise in the all-in Baa borrowing cost can come from either source, and each carries different sector implications. This distinguishes credit fear from a move in rates.",
          "sharpens": "When the spread is narrow but the Treasury yield is high, Baa borrowing costs are elevated because of rates rather than credit fear, which pressures rate-sensitive Utilities and Real Estate without signaling a crunch. When the spread is widening on top of a given Treasury yield, the extra cost is pure credit fear, which confirms a shift toward a stressed regime and favors defensives."
        },
        {
          "pairedMetric": "Net Charge-Off Rate, All Loans",
          "why": "The Baa spread prices expected future defaults on the borderline tier while the charge-off rate reports realized losses on bank loans, so reading them together shows whether the market's forward-looking fear is being confirmed by actual losses. Ziegel notes that reserves cover expected loss while capital covers unexpected loss, so realized charge-offs test whether the compensation the spread demanded was warranted. This links the priced risk to the realized outcome.",
          "sharpens": "When the spread widens while charge-offs stay low, the market is pricing trouble that has not yet arrived, which can be an early warning to lighten leveraged cyclicals. When the spread is elevated and charge-offs are rising together, the feared losses are materializing and eating bank capital, which confirms the stressed regime and strengthens the case for defensives."
        }
